WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.860
Picture one customer's account, acct-17, with two cards.

2
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.494
Each card holds 75.00.

3
00:00:08.494 --> 00:00:13.323
The rule is simple: the account's total must never go below zero.

4
00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:19.229
Two withdrawals of 100.00 arrive at the same moment, one from each card.

5
00:00:19.229 --> 00:00:22.059
Each checks the total first: 150.00.

6
00:00:22.059 --> 00:00:22.610
Plenty.

7
00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.025
So each takes 100.

8
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:25.268
Both commit.

9
00:00:25.268 --> 00:00:31.697
Card 1 is at -25.00, card 2 is at -25.00, and the account is 50.00 overdrawn.

10
00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:33.366
Nobody broke a lock.

11
00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:36.539
How did the database let this through?

12
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:39.415
Locks put the writers of one row in line.

13
00:00:39.415 --> 00:00:41.933
But look at what each withdrawal wrote.

14
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:43.419
The first wrote card 1.

15
00:00:43.419 --> 00:00:44.969
The second wrote card 2.

16
00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:47.552
Different rows, so no lock ever clashed.

17
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.628
Run them one after the other, and the second would have seen 50.00, and refused.

18
00:00:52.628 --> 00:00:57.456
Together, each made its decision from a total the other one was about to change.

19
00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:58.923
Each was careful.

20
00:00:58.923 --> 00:01:00.721
Just not with each other.

21
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:08.193
This is called write skew: two transactions read the same rows, then write different ones, and a rule breaks.

22
00:01:08.193 --> 00:01:09.689
Row locks can't see it.

23
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:12.424
What we want has a name: serializable.

24
00:01:12.424 --> 00:01:17.340
The result must equal running the transactions one at a time, in some order.

25
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:20.475
The first way to get it: make reads lock too.

26
00:01:20.475 --> 00:01:24.580
Every row a transaction reads gets a lock, held until it commits.

27
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:28.625
Now each withdrawal needs to write a card the other one holds.

28
00:01:28.625 --> 00:01:32.783
They wait for each other, the database stops one, and it runs again.

29
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:36.651
That's correct, and it's how MySQL's serializable works.

30
00:01:36.651 --> 00:01:38.654
But it brings the waiting back.

31
00:01:38.654 --> 00:01:44.275
A month-end report would lock 2 million rows, and every transfer would queue behind it.

32
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.667
So the wish: readers never wait, and still, somebody notices.

33
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:51.381
Picture an office archive with a clerk.

34
00:01:51.381 --> 00:01:54.105
Anyone can read any folder, with no waiting.

35
00:01:54.105 --> 00:01:57.819
But on the way out, you sign the folder's slip: I read this.

36
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.556
When someone changes a folder, the clerk checks its slip.

37
00:02:01.556 --> 00:02:06.769
Somebody else read the old one, so in any one-at-a-time story, that reader came first.

38
00:02:06.769 --> 00:02:09.619
The clerk draws an arrow: reader before writer.

39
00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:14.304
If the arrows ever go round in a circle, no one-at-a-time story fits.

40
00:02:14.304 --> 00:02:17.069
The clerk sends someone back to start over.

41
00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:17.816
Map it.

42
00:02:17.816 --> 00:02:22.503
The signature on the slip is a note PostgreSQL calls an SIREAD lock.

43
00:02:22.503 --> 00:02:25.053
The arrow is a read-write dependency.

44
00:02:25.053 --> 00:02:29.947
Being sent back is an error, and starting over is your program's retry.

45
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.830
Let's run the cards, under PostgreSQL's serializable.

46
00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:36.091
Both withdrawals read both cards.

47
00:02:36.091 --> 00:02:37.735
Each read leaves a note.

48
00:02:37.735 --> 00:02:39.379
The notes block nothing.

49
00:02:39.633 --> 00:02:42.468
The first withdrawal takes 100 from card 1.

50
00:02:42.468 --> 00:02:46.028
The second had read card 1, and won't see this change.

51
00:02:46.028 --> 00:02:48.731
So the second must come before the first.

52
00:02:48.731 --> 00:02:50.775
An arrow, from second to first.

53
00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:55.385
Now the second takes 100 from card 2, which the first had read.

54
00:02:55.385 --> 00:02:57.527
An arrow, from first to second.

55
00:02:57.527 --> 00:03:01.464
No row lock clashed, and still, the arrows make a circle.

56
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:02.886
The first commits.

57
00:03:02.886 --> 00:03:05.850
What happens when the second tries to commit?

58
00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:06.904
Pause and guess.

59
00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:14.316
It fails, with error 40001: could not serialize access.

60
00:03:14.316 --> 00:03:22.605
The second is a pivot: one arrow in, one arrow out, and the transaction at the far end has already committed.

61
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.396
The second withdrawal's change to card 2 is thrown away with it, so nobody will ever see it.

62
00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:32.077
The program's retry runs it again from the start.

63
00:03:32.077 --> 00:03:38.342
Its new snapshot sees card 1 at -25.00, the total is 50.00, and the withdrawal is refused.

64
00:03:38.342 --> 00:03:41.892
The same end as running the first, then the second.

65
00:03:42.167 --> 00:03:49.210
This is serializable snapshot isolation, SSI, PostgreSQL's serializable since version 9.1.

66
00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:52.575
Readers read their snapshot and never wait.

67
00:03:52.575 --> 00:03:54.923
The notes only feed the check.

68
00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.678
Searching every circle after each write would be slow.

69
00:03:58.678 --> 00:04:03.123
The theory behind it shows that every circle has two arrows in a row.

70
00:04:03.123 --> 00:04:08.340
So PostgreSQL only watches for a transaction with one arrow in and one arrow out.

71
00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.008
The shortcut has a cost: false alarms.

72
00:04:11.008 --> 00:04:18.107
The pattern can appear without a real circle, and notes on a whole table, instead of a row, make it more likely.

73
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:26.661
So under serializable, every transaction needs a retry loop: code that runs it again from the start after 40001.

74
00:04:26.661 --> 00:04:32.045
In exchange, you don't have to study every pair of transactions yourself.

75
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.891
So how did the account go 50.00 overdrawn?

76
00:04:34.891 --> 00:04:41.679
Each withdrawal decided from a total the other was changing, and they wrote different rows, so no lock saw it.

77
00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:47.873
Serializable snapshot isolation keeps notes of who read what, and sends one back.

78
00:04:47.873 --> 00:04:49.413
Nobody waits to read.

79
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.083
One quick check.

80
00:04:51.083 --> 00:04:54.876
Run the same two withdrawals at Repeatable Read instead.

81
00:04:54.876 --> 00:04:57.178
What are the two cards at the end?

82
00:04:57.178 --> 00:04:58.261
Pause and think.

83
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:02.359
-25.00 each.

84
00:05:02.359 --> 00:05:05.795
Both commit, and the account is 50.00 overdrawn.

85
00:05:05.795 --> 00:05:11.163
Repeatable Read gives each a snapshot, but keeps no notes of who read what.
